/target
# trybuild writes proposed expectations here when a compile-fail message
# changes. They are reviewed and moved into tests/ui/, never committed as-is.
wip/
.dust-extract/
# A vanilla server run directory, wherever an operator happens to leave one:
# the jars are Mojang's, the worlds are generated from them, and the logs are
# Mojang's too. None of it is this repository's to carry, and it is what both
# the protocol conformance test and the differential harness speak to. See
# crates/dust-protocol/tests/vanilla_conformance.rs.
.dust-vanilla/
# Dust's own run directory: `DEFAULT_WORLD_DIR`, relative to whatever directory
# the binary was started in. It holds `dust-edits.json` — the blocks players
# broke, where they stood, what the furnaces were smelting, and the world
# clock — written in the world-dirs teardown of every clean run. It is not
# `[server] world_source`, which is read-only region files an operator points
# at: the save is written whether or not one is set, and a flat run with no
# source at all leaves 129 bytes that put the clock back where it was on the
# next boot. Run state, not a build artifact, and none of it reproducible from
# what is committed here.
#
# Unanchored, because the working directory is the operator's and not this
# repository's. README's `cargo run -p dust-server -- server` leaves one at the
# root; `cargo run` from inside the crate left the one that reached commit
# ecbc1014 and sat in the tree two save-format versions stale.
world/
